>
  <p class="kk-dear">Hey,</p>
  <p>About 3 billion people open Facebook or Instagram every month. Some of them are in your city, and some of those are looking for exactly what you sell this week.</p>
  <p>The problem was never reaching them. The problem is that most ads go to everybody, and you pay for every one of them.</p>
  <p>If you'd like to know the simplest way we've found to use Facebook ads to get the phone ringing, and keep it ringing, then this is going to be the most useful thing you read today.</p>
  <p>Here's where we're coming from.</p>
  <p>We've run Facebook ads for over 40 businesses in 30 different niches across 20 states. Roofers, plumbers, auto shops, med spas, freight brokers, online stores.</p>
  <p>Here's one from last month. Parcel Management Group: 49 leads in 30 days at $17.70 a lead, from two campaigns.</p>
  <p>Here's a roofer. Gonzalez and Sons went from 2 roofs a month to 8.</p>
  <p>And look, when you run ads in that many different businesses, you learn what works. Not from a course. Not from some guy on YouTube with a ring light. From having to make the ads pay, because the client has a payroll to meet and so do we.</p>
  <p>And if you've run ads for any length of time, this is going to sound familiar.</p>
  <p>Cost per lead creeps up.</p>
  <p>The leads that do come in don't answer the phone.</p>
  <p>The one campaign that was working slows down and nobody knows why.</p>
  <p>So you pull back on the spend, because you can't see where it's going, and you tell yourself "ads don't work for my business."</p>
  <p>That's not an ads problem. That's a targeting problem and a follow-up problem, and we fix both.</p>
  <h2>THE THREE THINGS THAT ACTUALLY MATTER</h2>
  <h3>1. Who sees it</h3>
  <p>Most agencies pick an audience by checking boxes. Homeowners, 35 to 65, within 25 miles. Then they put an ad in front of all of them and hope.</p>
  <p>We start with Demand Intelligence. It watches recent search activity across 280 million U.S. consumer profiles, updated every day. You tell it what people look up before they buy from you. It gives you the list of people in your market who are looking right now. That list goes into Facebook as the audience.</p>
  <p>Same budget. A lot more of it lands on somebody who actually needs you this week.</p>
  <h3>2. What it looks like</h3>
  <p>The first thing most people do is try to make the ad "pop." Logo, brand colors, a stock photo of a guy in a polo. And they commit the one sin that kills every ad: they make it look like an ad.</p>
  <p>Don't make your ads look like ads. Make them look like the posts people are already stopping for. A real photo from a job. Your tech grimacing at what he found under the shingles. A customer text, with permission. That's what gets read, and when the ad gets read, Facebook shows it to more of the right people for less.</p>
  <h3>3. The words</h3>
  <p>When you get the words right, everything gets cheaper. Short sentences. One thought each. Sounds like you talking to a customer in the driveway, not like a brochure. Long copy wins, as long as it's interesting. And the first two lines have to make somebody hit "See more."</p>
  <p>Then a "Learn more" button. Not "Book now." The ad's only job is the click. The page and the phone call do the selling.</p>
  <p>Before you say "my business is different." We've done this in 30 niches. It's the same three things every time.</p>
  <h2>AND THEN THE PART EVERYBODY SKIPS</h2>
  <p>A lead that gets a call back in 5 minutes books. A lead that gets a call back tomorrow already hired the other guy.</p>
  <p>So every lead from the ads goes to a phone, not an inbox. A real person calls or texts inside five minutes. It gets logged in the CRM with a name on it. That's not a nice-to-have. It's written into our guarantee, because it's the single biggest thing we've seen separate the businesses that grow from the ones that quit.</p>
  <h2>WHY WE PUT IT IN WRITING</h2>
  <p>You've probably been promised results by somebody before. A pretty report every month, a lot of talk about "impressions," and a phone that didn't ring.</p>
  <p>So rather than tell you we're different, we put our own money on it. $50,000 in new revenue in 45-60 days, or you get your money back, plus $500 for wasting your time. Nobody else we know of is willing to write that down.</p>
  <p class="kk-ps">P.S. The whole Facebook playbook, the three things above and the thirty pages behind them, is in Marketing is F*cking Easy. It's free, further down this page. Read it, run it yourself, or hit the button and we'll run it for you.</p>
